    <h1>Salary vs Take-Home Pay: Understanding Taxes and Deductions</h1>
  </fragment>

  <p class="guide-date"><em>Last updated: February 2026</em></p>

  <h2>Your Salary Is Not Your Take-Home Pay</h2>
  <p>When you receive a job offer with a salary of $75,000, it is natural to assume you will have $75,000 deposited into your bank account over the course of the year. In reality, the amount you take home — your <strong>net pay</strong> — will be significantly lower than your <strong>gross salary</strong>. Understanding this gap is essential for budgeting, negotiating job offers, and planning your financial future.</p>
  <p>The difference between gross and net pay comes down to three main categories: <strong>federal income taxes</strong>, <strong>FICA taxes</strong> (Social Security and Medicare), and <strong>pre-tax deductions</strong> such as health insurance premiums and retirement contributions. State income taxes add another layer of complexity depending on where you live. Each of these elements chips away at your gross salary, and knowing how they work empowers you to make informed decisions.</p>
  <p>For a typical single filer earning $75,000 in 2026, the gap between gross and net pay can range from roughly 22% to over 35% depending on state taxes, benefits elections, and retirement contributions. Your actual take-home could be anywhere from $48,000 to $58,500 per year. The sections below break down each component so you can see exactly where your money goes.</p>

  <h2>Federal Income Tax Brackets</h2>
  <p>The United States uses a <strong>progressive income tax system</strong>, which means you pay higher rates on higher portions of your income. This is one of the most commonly misunderstood aspects of personal finance, yet it is critical for understanding your paycheck.</p>

  <h3>How Progressive Tax Brackets Work</h3>
  <p>Under a progressive system, your income is divided into chunks, and each chunk is taxed at a different rate. Earning more money never means you lose money to taxes overall — only the additional dollars above each threshold are taxed at the higher rate. This concept is called a <strong>marginal tax rate</strong>.</p>
  <p>For example, if you are a single filer in 2026 and your taxable income is $75,000, your income falls across multiple brackets. The first $11,925 is taxed at 10%. The portion from $11,926 to $48,475 is taxed at 12%. The portion from $48,476 to $75,000 is taxed at 22%. You do not pay 22% on your entire salary — only on the portion above $48,475.</p>

